    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Russian fleet had sailed seven months and 18,000 nautical miles from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, and it reached the strait with fouled hulls, crews with little training and a speed held to 9 knots by its slowest ships.</li><li>Japanese gunnery, high-explosive shells and wireless reports from scouting ships gave Tōgō the advantage, and four Russian battleships were sunk by sunset on 27 May.</li><li>Nearly the whole Russian fleet was sunk, captured or interned, and the Japanese lost three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> boats; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-portsmouth">Treaty of Portsmouth</a> followed in September 1905.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Oslyabya was the first modern armoured warship ever sunk by gunfire alone. One Japanese junior officer on the cruiser Nisshin, Takano Isoroku, lost two fingers in an accidental shell explosion and later became Admiral Yamamoto Isoroku.</p></aside>
    </section>

