    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">February 1904</span><span class="rf-text">Japanese destroyers attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-far-east">Russian Far East</a> Fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushunkou">Port Arthur</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1904</span><span class="rf-text">Rozhestvensky's squadron leaves Reval and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Libau</a> for the Far East, and the voyage takes it past the Dogger Bank, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cam-ranh-bay">Cam Ranh Bay</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1905</span><span class="rf-text">On 27 May the Japanese auxiliary cruiser Shinano Maru sights the Russian fleet and wireless reports reach Tōgō on the Mikasa</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1905</span><span class="rf-text">In the afternoon of 27 May Tōgō turns his line in sequence in the afternoon, Oslyabya, Knyaz Suvorov, Imperator Aleksandr III and Borodino are lost, and Rozhestvensky is knocked unconscious</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1905</span><span class="rf-text">From about 20:00 Japanese destroyers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> boats attack the scattered Russian ships through the night</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1905</span><span class="rf-text">On 28 May Nebogatov surrenders his remaining battleships and Tōgō accepts after the signal flags are understood</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1905</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-portsmouth">Treaty of Portsmouth</a> ends the war</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1905</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> begins construction of HMS Dreadnought</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-tsushima" data-v="9d2a5d0fb1" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The fleets sight each other</b> The Russians steamed north-north-east in columns, the main group on the right, the left column and the Nikolai I group to the west and the auxiliaries behind. The Japanese main group came down from the north-east heading south-south-west.</li><li><b>The Japanese turn in sequence and open fire</b> Mikasa turned about and the ships behind her followed in sequence to head north-north-east. The Russian main group had shifted left to cover the left column, and the Russians opened fire on the Japanese as they turned.</li><li><b>The Japanese cross ahead of the Russian column</b> Mikasa and her line turned north-east and then east to cross the T, and the Russian left column turned north-east and then east-south-east in response. Oslyabya was set on fire, and Knyaz Suvorov caught fire and fell away from the line.</li><li><b>Aleksandr III turns the Russian main group north</b> With the Japanese 1st division completely overtaking the Russian battleships heading east-south-east, Aleksandr III turned to the north, with Borodino and Oryol following.</li><li><b>The Japanese 1st division reverses; the 2nd attacks</b> The 1st division made an immediate left U-turn on the spot to head west-north-west, in reverse order. The 2nd division continued south-east and then south-west, attacking the secondary Russian warships.</li><li><b>Aleksandr III turns south-east</b> Aleksandr III, apparently giving up fleeing north, turned south-east, with Borodino and Oryol following. The Russian main group thus reversed its northward course and stood on the opposite heading to the Japanese line.</li><li><b>The 1st division loses sight of the main group</b> Nisshin and the five ships made another immediate left U-turn, and the 1st division headed north-east in normal order, with Mikasa first and Nisshin last. The 1st division lost sight of the Russian main group in the smoke and mist.</li><li><b>The 2nd division finds the main group</b> The Japanese 2nd division found the Russian main group close to where the 3rd Squadron, the 5th and 6th divisions, was attacking the Russian auxiliaries protected by cruisers.</li><li><b>The Japanese main group catches the Russian main group</b> After losing sight of the Russian main group in smoke and mist, the 1st division caught up with it heading north-west and fired on the leading Aleksandr III. The 5th division, part of the 3rd Squadron, had been attacking the Russian auxiliaries.</li><li><b>Torpedo attacks in the night</b> The 1st division left the battleground and the 2nd and 4th were ordered to gather in the north, leaving the destroyers and torpedo boats to attack in the dark. The attacks forced the Russians to turn west, and they were dispersed in small groups.</li><li><b>The Russian remnant is sighted and surrounded</b> The remaining Russian ships were sighted heading north-east. Tōgō's battleships moved to surround Nebogatov's remaining squadron south of the island of Takeshima, the 1st division approaching from the north.</li><li><b>Nebogatov surrenders</b> The Japanese battleships opened main battery fire at 12,000 metres. Nebogatov signalled surrender at 10:34, and Tōgō accepted it at 10:45 once the Russian ships had stopped.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war.jpg" alt="Russo-Japanese War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RUSSOJAPANESEWARIMAGE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original uploader and author was Dustandshadow at en.wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> began on 8 February 1904, when Japanese destroyers attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-far-east">Russian Far East</a> Fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushunkou">Port Arthur</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s first aim was to secure its supply lines to the Asian mainland for a ground war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, which meant neutralising Russian naval power in the Far East. After the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a> on 10 August, where Admiral Wilgelm Vitgeft was killed, and the Battle off Ulsan on 14 August 1904, what remained of Russian Pacific naval power was sunk in Port Arthur in December 1904.</p><p>The Russians then considered sending part of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-fleet">Baltic Fleet</a> east. The plan was to relieve Port Arthur by sea, join the First Pacific Squadron, overwhelm the Japanese Navy and delay the Japanese advance in Manchuria until reinforcements could arrive by the Trans-Siberian railway. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> agreed to form the Second Pacific Squadron, five divisions of the Baltic Fleet including 11 of its 13 battleships. The Admiralty Council had opposed the dispatch, arguing that the Japanese crews all had combat experience and that training in the tropics would be practically impossible. At a council on 23 August 1904 Rozhestvensky, Navy Minister Avellan and the Tsar overruled it, since the coaling contract with a German shipping line was already signed.</p><p>Port Arthur fell on 2 January 1905, just after the squadron arrived at Nossi Be in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, so its objective became to join the ships at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a> before bringing the Japanese fleet to battle.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Tsushima&#8221; cites where it covers Conflict in the Far East, Departure, Dogger Bank, Routes:</p><ul><li>Lawrence Sondhaus, Naval Warfare, 1815–1914 (Routledge, 2001)</li><li>Robert Forczyk, Russian Battleship vs Japanese Battleship, Yellow Sea 1904–1905 (Osprey, 2009)</li><li>Konstantin Pleshakov, The Tsar's last armada: the epic journey to the Battle of Tsushima (Basic Books, 2002)</li><li>H. P. Willmott, The Last Century of Sea Power: From Port Arthur to Chanak, 1894–1922, Volume 1 (Indiana University Press, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Russian fleet had sailed over seven months and 18,000 nautical miles from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. At the Dogger Bank it had fired on British fishing trawlers, killing several fishermen, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> almost entered the war in support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The fleet waited about two months each at Nosy Be in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cam-ranh-bay">Cam Ranh Bay</a>, and its hulls were heavily fouled. The four new Borodino-class battleships had little time for crew training, and the rest of Nebogatov's squadron was older and poorly maintained. Russian ships had a top speed of 14 to 15 knots, and the auxiliaries 10 to 12, which limited the fleet to 9 knots. The Russians had a large advantage in battleships but were outnumbered nearly three to one in total hulls.</p><p>Tōgō's Combined Fleet put to sea with over 40 vessels. His 1st Battle Division could exceed 18 knots, and he had combat experience that Rozhestvensky lacked. His four modern battleships carried 16 Armstrong 12-inch guns with a range of 14 km and a rate of fire of one shot a minute, against 20 of the 1895 12-inch guns on five Russian battleships, with a range of 11 km and a rate of fire of 2.5 to 3 minutes in practice. The Japanese used high-explosive shells with Shimose powder, and a central fire control system introduced by Katō Hiroharu, while the Russians still used older armour-piercing rounds with guncotton charges.</p><p>The Russians had three possible routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a>. Rozhestvensky revealed his choice of the Tsushima Strait only on 25 May, and the fleet approached in radio silence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 27 May, in fog, the Japanese auxiliary cruiser Shinano Maru saw the lights of the Russian hospital ship Orel and then the shapes of ten other ships. At 04:55 she wired the Combined Fleet command, and Tōgō received the message at 05:05. At 06:34 he wired Tokyo that the fleet would attack and destroy the enemy, adding that the weather was fine but the waves high. Scouting vessels sent wireless reports every few minutes. The fleets sighted each other at 13:40, and about 13:55 Tōgō hoisted the Z flag: the fate of the Empire depended on the result of the battle.</p><p>The Russians sailed from south-southwest to north-northeast and the Japanese from northeast to southwest. Tōgō ordered a 180-degree turn in sequence, and Rozhestvensky chose a formal pitched battle. At 14:08 the Mikasa was hit at about 7,000 metres, and she was hit 15 times in five minutes, but superior Japanese gunnery crippled most Russian battleships in thirty minutes. Oslyabya was the first sunk. By 14:45 Tōgō had crossed the Russian T. A hit on the magazines of Borodino by the battleship Fuji made her explode, and a shell fragment struck Rozhestvensky's skull. Nebogatov took command in the evening, the Russians having lost Knyaz Suvorov, Oslyabya, Imperator Aleksandr III and Borodino.</p><p>At about 20:00, 21 destroyers and 45 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> boats attacked for three hours. The battleship Navarin was torpedoed four times and sunk, and only three of 622 crew survived. Sissoi Veliky was scuttled the next day, and the armoured cruisers Admiral Nakhimov and Vladimir Monomakh were scuttled by their crews.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Tsushima&#8221; cites where it covers First contact, Beginning of the battle, Daylight action, Night attacks:</p><ul><li>Captain Vladimir Ivanovich Semenoff, The Battle of Tsu-Shima (John Murray, 1907)</li><li>Ryōtarō Shiba, Clouds Above the Hill (Routledge, 2014)</li><li>Mark Lardas, Tsushima 1905: Death of a Russian Fleet (Bloomsbury Publishing, 2018)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At 05:23 on 28 May the Japanese sighted the remaining Russian ships heading northeast and surrounded Nebogatov's squadron, opening fire at 12,000 metres. The cruiser Izumrud fled. Nebogatov ordered his four battleships to surrender, knowing his guns were outranged and his ships slower. He hoisted XGE, the international signal of surrender, but the Japanese continued to fire because their code books lacked a signal for surrender, and a white tablecloth was then raised. Tōgō knew the signal meant a request for a truce, and his officers found the code book and reported that the engines had to stop. Nebogatov stopped all engines, and Tōgō ceased fire. Nebogatov told his men that the lives of the two thousand four hundred men in the ships were more important than his own.</p><p>Rozhestvensky, rescued from Knyaz Suvorov, had been moved to the destroyer Byedoviy, which raised a white flag when two Japanese destroyers caught it. He was taken to hospital in Sasebo. Until the evening of 28 May the Japanese pursued isolated Russian ships until almost all were destroyed or captured.</p><p>All 11 Russian battleships were lost, seven sunk and four captured. One tally gives 216 officers and 4,614 men killed and 278 officers and 5,629 men taken prisoner. Another gives 4,380 killed and 5,917 captured, with 1,862 interned. Three cruisers were interned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>. The Japanese lost three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> boats, with 117 killed and 583 wounded. Rozhestvensky and Nebogatov were sentenced to death at trials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1905 and the sentences were commuted. Nebogatov was released in May 1909.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Tsushima&#8221; cites where it covers Battle damage and casualties, Russian losses, Political consequences, Dreadnought arms race:</p><ul><li>H. P. Willmott, The Last Century of Sea Power: From Port Arthur to Chanak, 1894–1922, Volume 1 (Indiana University Press, 2009)</li><li>Lawrence Sondhaus, Naval Warfare, 1815–1914 (Routledge, 2001)</li><li>Frank Thiess, Tsushima (Rowohlt Verlag, 1954)</li><li>Konstantin Pleshakov, The Tsar's last armada: the epic journey to the Battle of Tsushima (Basic Books, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known through the reports and recollections of men who took part on both sides, and through a minute-by-minute timetable kept in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> Standard Time. Admiral Tōgō's own report describes how wireless let the Japanese follow the Russians' movements at a distance of 48 to 64 km through fog. Captain Vladimir Semenoff, a Russian staff officer aboard Knyaz Suvorov, described the shellfire that struck the flagship. Captain William Pakenham of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> was present aboard the Japanese battleship Asahi as an official observer, and British intelligence also followed the Japanese fleet's training in the months before.</p><p>Estimates of Russian losses differ: one gives more than 5,000 dead and 6,000 captured, another 4,380 killed and 5,917 captured, and a third 216 officers and 4,614 men killed with 278 officers and 5,629 men captured. The causes of the Japanese victory are argued through a list of factors: experience, the salvo firing director, wireless, British support, high-explosive shells and gun range. Geoffrey Regan thought the result misleading, because the Japanese navy had performed well but its opponents were weak.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji.jpg" alt="Emperor Meiji" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meiji_Emperor_(cropped)(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uchida Kuichi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle was hailed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as one of the greatest naval victories in its history, and Tōgō was revered as a national hero. His flagship Mikasa is preserved as a museum ship in Yokosuka Harbour. He was appointed to the Order of Merit by King Edward VII on 21 February 1906 and was made an earl by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a> in 1907. The battle has been the focus of two Japanese films, from 1969 and 1983, and appears in the NHK series Saka no Ue no Kumo. It has been called the only decisive engagement ever fought between modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> battleship fleets and the first in which wireless telegraphy played a critically important role.</p><p>It was the first defeat of a European power by an Asian nation in the modern era. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> were among the future national leaders who celebrated this defeat of a colonial power. Japan was established as the sixth greatest naval power and the Russian navy declined to one barely stronger than that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. Barbara Tuchman argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s loss destabilised the balance of power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and contributed to the decision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> to go to war in 1914. Geoffrey Regan argues that the victory created a legend that haunted Japan's leaders for forty years and set Japan on a path towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p>Admiral Jackie Fisher reasoned that Tsushima confirmed the importance of large guns and speed, and in October 1905 the British began construction of HMS Dreadnought, which marked the beginning of a naval arms race between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Tsushima on 27–28 May 1905 the Japanese fleet under Tōgō sank or captured almost all of the Russian squadron that had sailed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, losing three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> boats. The defeat forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to seek peace, and the Japanese navy's victory was widely celebrated.</em></p>

